(load-lain "environments/catalog-discovery.lain")
(define directed-role "You are constructing executable Lain in a fresh process. Only catalog metadata is loaded; there are NO live saved callables yet. First query metadata, then choose an applicable current identity by connecting its keyword to selected-id and your reasons to selection-notes. Lain will load that identity's saved source dependencies and admit them in lexical children. Wait for the execute stage before catalog:resolve. Do not regenerate a saved implementation. Source reconstruction restores definitions, not old input values or old user replies. On the execute stage the selected callable is live. All requested output ports belong to the parent: connect with ->; never shadow them with define. Raw complete Lain forms only; no Markdown or prose outside strings. Output format example: valid response is (define example-value (+ 2 3)). This illustrates raw syntax only; use the output port requested by the current task. Invalid response is ```lain followed by code and closing ```, or an explanation before the forms. Your response must begin with ( and contain only complete Lain forms. Never use backticks.")
(define directed-catalog-guide "catalog:register takes an EDN metadata string and a cell reference, and returns a receipt. Example: (catalog:register \"{:catalog/id :example/v1 :catalog/description \\\"Multiply quantity by rate\\\" :catalog/interface \\\"[quantity rate] -> amount\\\" :catalog/assumptions \\\"Compatible units\\\" :catalog/status :current :catalog/tag [:linear] :catalog/capability [[:catalog/id :cap/linear]]}\" (cell:reference f)). Use normal escaped string quotes in actual source. Registration records model claims, not proof of correctness. Repeating identical registration is idempotent; conflicting identities are rejected. catalog:resolve takes a registered keyword id and returns its LIVE CELL REFERENCE; example (define candidate (cell:value (catalog:resolve :example/v1))) then (candidate 4 3). Saved source is restored by Lain after you publish selected-id; metadata discovery alone does not create live references. catalog:query takes EDN query text and EDN rules text and returns rows. Always use :in $ %. Example: (catalog:query \"[:find ?id :in $ % :where [?n :catalog/id ?id] [?n :catalog/status :current]]\" \"[]\"). Only relation queries, fact clauses and named rule calls are needed. No eval or host code. Results have at most 64 rows. Search and inspect applicability before constructing a duplicate; you may reject unsuitable candidates. Exact database schema (Datomic Local): :catalog/id is a unique keyword identity. :catalog/description, :catalog/interface and :catalog/assumptions are strings. :catalog/status is a keyword such as :current or :superseded. :catalog/tag is a many-valued keyword attribute. :catalog/capability and :catalog/depends-on are many-valued ENTITY REFERENCES, not keyword values. For example, [?root :catalog/id :cap/event-cost] binds the internal entity id to ?root; [?root :catalog/depends-on ?next] binds another internal entity id. To obtain its public keyword, join [?next :catalog/id ?next-id]. Registration lookup refs like [:catalog/id :cap/linear] resolve to those internal entity ids. All registered candidates have description/interface/assumptions/status. Do not guess alternate storage shapes. The supported subset is :find variables :in $ % :where triples and named-rule calls. No or-join, arbitrary predicates, aggregates, pull, or host functions. A transitive relation operates on entity ids. Multiple rules with the same head provide alternatives. Query only the supplied schema. The database has a :catalog/example string attribute on each operator. Retrieve it alongside description, interface, assumptions, status and identity. Examples are documentation; they do not execute and are not proof. Check them by invoking the discovered callable. Resolve a returned public id with catalog:resolve; cell:value obtains the live callable. The catalog starts with independent general-purpose operators. It can accumulate new callable registrations during this session. A composed closure can capture earlier callable values, while exposing varying domain quantities as parameters. Register it with a fresh identity, description, interface, assumptions, status, capability and a concrete :catalog/example. Do not capture the current question inputs into a supposedly general callable. Source from earlier stages is not supplied; the database is your discovery route.")
(define directed-search-task "Query all documented operator candidates with id, description, interface, assumptions, status and example. Include obsolete candidates for comparison. Use catalog:query with :in $ % and rules text \"[]\"; connect actual rows to discovery-results. Discovery only: do not resolve callables or compute a price. Return only raw Lain forms, beginning with (. No Markdown code fences or commentary. Required wiring example: (define candidates (catalog:query query-text rules-text)) (-> candidates discovery-results). Name your local query result candidates or another fresh name. Incorrect: (define discovery-results ...) shadows the parent and prevents continuation. Do not connect these rows to answer; answer is only for the later numeric result.")
(define directed-select-new "A new quote requires priced pieces, priced labor and a fractional surcharge on their combined cost, followed by a fixed delivery charge that is NOT surcharged. Choose the best existing whole-event callable as a reusable prerequisite for this extension. Inspect memory-cell rows and reject obsolete or incompatible candidates. Connect the chosen public keyword to selected-id and explain selection/rejections in selection-notes. Do not resolve, invoke or register yet; Lain restores after this stage. Output wiring example (choose your actual discovered identity): (-> :example/v1 selected-id) (-> \"Applicability and rejection reasons\" selection-notes). Incorrect: (define selected-id ...) or (define selection-notes ...) creates a local shadow, and (-> selected-id selected-id) connects that local cell to itself rather than the parent. Never define either output name. Return raw forms without code fences.")
(define directed-select-reuse "A later order requires priced pieces, priced labor and a fractional surcharge on their combined cost, followed by a fixed delivery charge that is NOT surcharged. Choose the most specific applicable stored callable for the whole delivered quote from memory-cell rows. Connect its public keyword to selected-id and selection/rejection reasons to selection-notes. Do not resolve or invoke until the execute stage; Lain restores your selection first. Output wiring example (choose your actual discovered identity): (-> :example/v1 selected-id) (-> \"Applicability and rejection reasons\" selection-notes). Incorrect: (define selected-id ...) or (define selection-notes ...) creates a local shadow, and (-> selected-id selected-id) connects that local cell to itself rather than the parent. Never define either output name. Return raw forms without code fences.")
(define directed-compose-task "The selected identity in memory-cell has now been restored. Resolve that identity literally and observe its callable with cell:value. Construct and register a reusable closure for a delivered quote: the whole-event quote plus a fixed delivery charge AFTER surcharge. Expose six parameters quantity, unit-price, hours, hourly-rate, surcharge-rate, delivery-charge; capture the restored callable, not these input cells. Inside the closure actually invoke that callable. Choose a fresh catalog identity, document interface, assumptions, status :current, a numeric example, and capability [[:catalog/id :cap/delivered-event-cost]]. Bind the catalog:register receipt and connect it to cumulative-ready. Invoke the new closure on the six parent inputs. Connect computed number to answer and computed text to user-output. All outputs use ->; never redefine a parent port. Do not implement a later question or its network. Concrete output-wiring example, assuming the chosen callable is bound to f: (define total (f quantity unit-price hours hourly-rate surcharge-rate delivery-charge)) (-> total answer) (-> (str \"Total: \" total) user-output). Incorrect: (define answer ...) or (define user-output ...) makes a local cell and leaves the parent output unknown. Use a fresh local name such as total for your computed value, then use the two -> connections. Return raw Lain forms without fences.")
(define directed-reuse-task "The selected whole delivered-quote callable in memory-cell is now restored. Resolve its literal public identity, observe the callable using cell:value, and invoke its documented six-argument interface on quantity, unit-price, hours, hourly-rate, surcharge-rate, delivery-charge. Connect computed number to answer and computed text to user-output. Do not register a new closure or reconstruct the stored formula. Input changes must propagate without another model call. Concrete output-wiring example, assuming the chosen callable is bound to f: (define total (f quantity unit-price hours hourly-rate surcharge-rate delivery-charge)) (-> total answer) (-> (str \"Total: \" total) user-output). Incorrect: (define answer ...) or (define user-output ...) makes a local cell and leaves the parent output unknown. Use a fresh local name such as total for your computed value, then use the two -> connections. Return raw Lain forms without fences.")
(define selected-id)
(define delivery-charge)
(define cumulative-ready)
(define accumulate-mode)
(define directed-seed (let [e0 seed
e1 (add-binding e0 "role-cell" (cell:reference directed-role))
e2 (add-binding e1 "catalog-guide" (cell:reference directed-catalog-guide))
e3 (add-binding e2 "selected-id" (cell:reference selected-id))
e4 (add-binding e3 "delivery-charge" (cell:reference delivery-charge))
e5 (add-binding e4 "cumulative-ready" (cell:reference cumulative-ready))
e6 (add-binding e5 "accumulate-mode" (cell:reference accumulate-mode))
] e6))
